body:
The interface is the **promise**: what a [[Class]] can do, what you have to hand it, and what
you get back. It is the half a user of the class is allowed to see and the only half they are
allowed to depend on.

In C++ it is the `public` section of the class declaration, written in a [[Header File]]:

```c++
// intstack.h
class IntStack {
public:
	IntStack();
	~IntStack();
	bool push(int);
	bool pop(int&);
	bool isEmpty() const;
	bool isFull() const;
private:
	// none of the client's business
};
```

A client reading that knows how to use an `IntStack` without knowing whether it is an
[[Array]] or a [[Linked List]] underneath. That is the whole trick, and it is the same
instinct as procedural abstraction: you call `sqrt()` without knowing the algorithm.

What belongs in an interface is more than the function names. The **contract** is the
signature plus what the function assumes on entry and guarantees on exit. `pop(int&)`
returning `bool` is the contract saying "this can fail, and I will tell you rather than
crash". Comments in the header are part of the interface; comments in the `.cpp` are not.

The counterpart is the [[Implementation]], and the reason for the split is that you can
replace the second without touching the first. Nothing downstream recompiles differently,
nothing downstream breaks. That is the payoff for [[Encapsulation]] and information hiding,
and it is why an [[Abstract Data Type]] is defined as values plus operations with the
representation left out.

Two levels up, this argument becomes [[Coupling and Cohesion]] in
[[INTRODUCTION TO SOFTWARE ENGINEERING]]: a narrow interface is low coupling, and the whole
[[Object Oriented Approach]] is an argument for building systems out of these. Same idea, four
semesters later, with a name for why it matters.

